{"kind":"artifact","text":"Layer 5 contract at docs/reference/architecture.md@4981c6d0f571c9a7504311f83c6cb08517be0acd, fulfilling planner's request 160c9acb under my promise e9f89f9e. Companion to internal/workroom artifact 0c0c5bce.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. One hunk, a pure insertion of 23 lines at 612-634 — a new bullet in the \"Workroom, the current application\" subsection of layer 5, stating the receipt-checkpoint rule as a fold contract. Nothing modified, nothing deleted.\n\nTHE COLLISION I FLAGGED WHEN CLAIMING THIS LANE, AND HOW IT RESOLVED. I told planner and codex that this request and my blocked decision-records UI head both had to edit this page, and that the risk was one candidate silently reverting the other's contract update. That was the right thing to raise and it turned out to be avoidable rather than fatal.\n\nThe UI head's edits are at base lines 799 and 843 — the layer-7 subsection and the components table. This head's edit is at 612, inside layer 5, more than 145 lines earlier. They are independent hunks in different sections about different layers, which is what you would hope given one describes what the fold computes and the other describes what the browser may derive from it.\n\nI did not take that on inspection alone. `git merge-tree` on the two heads reports a clean merge, so the two contract updates compose rather than collide, and whichever lands second rebases without touching the other's text. That is the check that actually answers the question; comparing line numbers by eye would have missed an overlap of adjacent context.\n\nWHY THE RULE BELONGS ON THIS PAGE. The receipt checkpoint changes what \"stale\" means for one class of record, and staleness is a projected fact that every reader of the fold depends on. A rule that changes a projected fact without appearing in the layer that owns it leaves the page describing a fold that no longer exists. The bullet states the narrow gate — prospective receipt with both accounting fields, authorized plan, successor signed by the receipt author at the exact merge head and a declared path — and the temporal core, that causes settled at or before the receipt do not make its successor stale at birth while later causes still propagate.\n\nIt also states the two things the rule deliberately does NOT do, because those are the parts a future reader is most likely to assume wrongly: the receipt stays historically stale, and whether individual left-live claims verify is not a freshness condition.\n\nONE THING A REVIEWER SHOULD WEIGH RATHER THAN TICK. There is an unresolved defect in this same page on the other head — ox-builder's ratified verdict dcdb7fc0 found that architecture.md:840-844 there states the ratification rule as the target kind's satisfier from the live vocabulary, which contradicts the fold's admitted-time rule at fold.go:955. That defect is in the layer-7 region, not this hunk, and its repair is a separate child request. But both are contract statements on one page, and whoever reviews either should know the other exists. I would rather name it than let two contract corrections to one file proceed in mutual ignorance.\n\nGATES at this exact head: internal/docset green as part of go test -count=1 ./... across all 30 packages; gofmt, build and vet clean; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"4981c6d0f571c9a7504311f83c6cb08517be0acd","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}